## Propagating Trace Context Through RabbitMQ Headers

The key is to manually inject/extract the W3C trace context into RabbitMQ message headers using `System.Diagnostics` and OpenTelemetry's propagator.

### Publisher (Service A)

```csharp
using System.Diagnostics;
using OpenTelemetry;
using OpenTelemetry.Context.Propagation;
using RabbitMQ.Client;

private static readonly ActivitySource ActivitySource = new("ServiceA.Messaging");
private static readonly TextMapPropagator Propagator = Propagators.DefaultTextMapPropagator;

public void PublishOrder(Order order)
{
    using var activity = ActivitySource.StartActivity("order.publish", ActivityKind.Producer);

    var properties = channel.CreateBasicProperties();
    properties.Headers ??= new Dictionary<string, object>();

    // Inject current trace context into message headers
    Propagator.Inject(
        new PropagationContext(activity.Context, Baggage.Current),
        properties.Headers,
        (headers, key, value) => headers[key] = value);

    var body = JsonSerializer.SerializeToUtf8Bytes(order);
    channel.BasicPublish(exchange: "", routingKey: "orders", basicProperties: properties, body: body);
}
```

### Consumer (Service B)

```csharp
using System.Diagnostics;
using OpenTelemetry;
using OpenTelemetry.Context.Propagation;

private static readonly ActivitySource ActivitySource = new("ServiceB.Messaging");
private static readonly TextMapPropagator Propagator = Propagators.DefaultTextMapPropagator;

private void HandleMessage(object sender, BasicDeliverEventArgs ea)
{
    // Extract trace context from message headers
    var parentContext = Propagator.Extract(
        default,
        ea.BasicProperties.Headers,
        (headers, key) =>
        {
            if (headers != null && headers.TryGetValue(key, out var value))
                return new[] { Encoding.UTF8.GetString((byte[])value) };
            return Enumerable.Empty<string>();
        });

    Baggage.Current = parentContext.Baggage;

    // Start a linked activity using the extracted context
    using var activity = ActivitySource.StartActivity(
        "order.process",
        ActivityKind.Consumer,
        parentContext.ActivityContext);

    // Process message — this activity is now part of the same trace
    ProcessOrder(ea.Body);
}
```

### OpenTelemetry Setup (both services)

```csharp
builder.Services.AddOpenTelemetry()
    .WithTracing(tracing => tracing
        .AddSource("ServiceA.Messaging")  // or ServiceB.Messaging
        .AddAspNetCoreInstrumentation()
        .AddOtlpExporter());
```

### Why this works

RabbitMQ doesn't propagate context automatically. The `Propagator.Inject` call writes `traceparent` and `tracestate` W3C headers into the message properties. On the consumer side, `Propagator.Extract` reads them back and the new `Activity` is created as a child of that context — linking both services into one distributed trace.

> **Tip:** If you use [MassTransit](https://masstransit.io/) or [NServiceBus](https://docs.particular.net/), they handle this propagation automatically. The manual approach above is for raw `RabbitMQ.Client` usage.
